109
00:06:51.392 --> 00:06:53.269
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

110
00:06:54.519 --> 00:06:55.437
Intuition.

111
00:06:55.717 --> 00:06:59.045
An inverted index is the index at the back of a textbook.

112
00:06:59.325 --> 00:07:04.679
Instead of reading every page for paneer, you look up paneer and get page numbers (postings).

113
00:07:04.959 --> 00:07:11.210
L1: looking up the index was 194x faster than scanning 20,000 products.

114
00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.498
Analysis is agreeing on spelling before looking up.

115
00:07:14.778 --> 00:07:20.261
Aata, atta and आटा must end up as the same index entry, or the lookup misses.

116
00:07:20.541 --> 00:07:25.447
The same applies to 5kg and 5 kg, and to tomatoes and tomato.

117
00:07:26.797 --> 00:07:29.549
Relevance ranking is the shopkeeper's judgement.

118
00:07:29.829 --> 00:07:33.797
Of the 2,000 items that mention oil, which 10 do you show first?

119
00:07:34.077 --> 00:07:39.709
Rarer words matter more (IDF), and repeated words matter less each time (saturation).

120
00:07:39.989 --> 00:07:43.573
Then business sense applies: in stock first, popular first.

121
00:07:44.923 --> 00:07:46.203
The inverted index.

122
00:07:46.483 --> 00:07:47.379
Structure:

123
00:07:47.659 --> 00:07:51.521
A term dictionary (sorted terms, often an FST in Lucene).

124
00:07:51.801 --> 00:07:59.694
For each term, a postings list of documents (plus term frequencies, positions for phrase queries, and offsets for highlighting).

125
00:07:59.974 --> 00:08:04.646
Postings are compressed (delta + bit packing) and intersected with skip lists.

126
00:08:05.996 --> 00:08:06.849
Segments:

127
00:08:07.129 --> 00:08:10.073
Lucene-based engines write immutable segments.

128
00:08:10.353 --> 00:08:20.209
New documents go into a new segment, which becomes searchable after a refresh (1 s by default in Elasticsearch or OpenSearch, hence near-real-time).

129
00:08:20.489 --> 00:08:25.268
Deletes are tombstones, and background merges combine segments and purge deletes.

130
00:08:25.548 --> 00:08:32.247
Doc values or columnar fields support sorting, facets and aggregations without the inverted index.

131
00:08:33.597 --> 00:08:38.930
Sharding: an index is split into shards (hash of id by default) and replicated.

132
00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:44.330
A query fans out to all shards and merges the top-k (scatter-gather, Ch 11).

133
00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:53.527
L1's toy index for 20,000 products has 160 terms and 113,296 postings.

134
00:08:53.807 --> 00:08:58.010
Real catalogs have millions of terms, but the mechanism is the same.

135
00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:01.771
Analysis: tokenizers and filters.

136
00:09:02.051 --> 00:09:14.424
An analyzer is a character filter, then tokenizer, then token filters, applied at index time and at query time (usually the same one, sometimes different, as with synonyms or edge n-grams).

