174
00:11:04.167 --> 00:11:05.575
Zero-result queries:

175
00:11:05.855 --> 00:11:10.186
0. + product-type field (boost 3). nDCG@10:

176
00:11:10.466 --> 00:11:12.258
1.000.

177
00:11:12.538 --> 00:11:13.946
Zero-result queries:

178
00:11:14.226 --> 00:11:14.973
0.

179
00:11:16.323 --> 00:11:25.795
Stemming versus lemmatisation: stemmers (Porter or Snowball) chop suffixes by rule (tomatoes, then tomato, fine; news, then new, wrong).

180
00:11:26.075 --> 00:11:30.384
PostgreSQL's english config and Lucene's analyzers use Snowball.

181
00:11:30.664 --> 00:11:36.083
For product names, light stemming (plurals only) is usually safer than aggressive stemming.

182
00:11:37.433 --> 00:11:40.270
Indian languages, transliteration and Hinglish.

183
00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:43.963
Indian grocery search mixes three forms of the same word:

184
00:11:44.243 --> 00:11:45.374
English (flour);.

185
00:11:45.654 --> 00:11:49.771
Hindi in Latin script, spelled inconsistently (aata, atta, ata);.

186
00:11:50.051 --> 00:11:51.971
Devanagari (आटा).

187
00:11:52.251 --> 00:11:56.795
Regional languages (Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, Bengali) add more scripts.

188
00:11:57.075 --> 00:11:58.014
The techniques:

189
00:11:59.364 --> 00:12:03.588
Transliteration to a common script at both index and query time.

190
00:12:03.868 --> 00:12:08.732
L1 maps Devanagari to Latin (ITRANS-like, with the word-final schwa dropped).

191
00:12:09.012 --> 00:12:14.815
ICU's Devanagari-Latin transform or IndicNLP libraries do this properly.

192
00:12:15.095 --> 00:12:27.681
Phonetic normalisation of Latin Hinglish spellings: collapse long vowels (aa, then a, ee, then i, oo, then u) and double consonants, and map w, then v, z, then j, ph, then f.

193
00:12:27.961 --> 00:12:36.132
L1: आटा, aata and Atta all become ata. दूध and doodh become dudh. मूंग and moong become mung.

194
00:12:36.412 --> 00:12:42.897
Phonetic algorithms like Soundex or Metaphone are designed for English names and do poorly on Hinglish.

195
00:12:44.247 --> 00:12:51.266
Synonyms bridging languages (milk ↔ doodh, curd ↔ dahi, sugar ↔ chini).

196
00:12:51.546 --> 00:12:59.034
They're applied at query time so they can change without reindexing, and they're keyed on the normalised form so all spellings match.

197
00:12:59.314 --> 00:13:08.039
Index-time enrichment: add an aliases field per product type (aata atta आटा gehun flour), maintained by merchandising.

198
00:13:08.319 --> 00:13:16.575
In L3 this took a stock engine from 0.779 to 0.979 without writing an analyzer.

199
00:13:16.855 --> 00:13:17.687
Limits:

200
00:13:19.037 --> 00:13:26.163
Medial schwa deletion is irregular: नमकीन transliterates to namakin, but people type namkeen.

201
00:13:26.443 --> 00:13:30.539
So transliteration plus fuzziness or aliases are complementary.

202
00:13:30.819 --> 00:13:36.621
Neither replaces a query log review: the top zero-result queries each week are the to-do list.

203
00:13:37.971 --> 00:13:38.867
Relevance:

204
00:13:39.147 --> 00:13:41.985
TF-IDF, BM25, field boosts.

205
00:13:42.265 --> 00:13:50.990
BM25 (the default in Lucene, Elasticsearch or OpenSearch and most engines) scores a document for a query term t:

206
00:13:52.340 --> 00:14:02.196
On screen: score(t, d) = IDF(t), tf, (k1 + 1) / (tf + k1, (1 minus b + b, d/avgdl)).

207
00:14:02.476 --> 00:14:10.391
IDF(t) = ln(1 + (N minus df + 0.5)/(df + 0.5)).

208
00:14:13.241 --> 00:14:15.033
IDF: rare terms matter more.

209
00:14:15.313 --> 00:14:17.275
Paneer discriminates more than fresh.

210
00:14:17.555 --> 00:14:22.675
TF saturation (k1, ~1.2): the 5th occurrence adds little.

211
00:14:22.955 --> 00:14:30.891
Length normalisation (b, ~0.75): a match in a short name counts more than one in a long description.

212
00:14:31.171 --> 00:14:36.547
Field boosts (BM25F): a match in name counts more than one in description.

213
00:14:36.827 --> 00:14:43.995
L1 uses name times 2, brand or category or size times 1, and a curated type field times 3.

214
00:14:44.275 --> 00:14:45.641
Query-side choices:

215
00:14:46.991 --> 00:15:04.185
AND versus OR, minimumshouldmatch;. phrase and proximity boosts (toor dal as a phrase);. multimatch types (bestfields versus crossfields);. scoring each expansion (synonym or fuzzy) as the max, not the sum, so an expanded term doesn't count twice (L1).

216
00:15:05.535 --> 00:15:06.837
Query understanding.

217
00:15:07.117 --> 00:15:10.466
Most relevance problems are about understanding the query:

218
00:15:10.746 --> 00:15:21.370
Attribute extraction: paneer 200g: the product is paneer and the size is 200 g. atta 5kg, then type atta, size 5 kg.

219
00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:28.327
L1's unit normaliser turns 5 kg, 5kg and 5 kgs into one token.

220
00:15:28.607 --> 00:15:33.578
Product-type or category intent: potato is a vegetable query, not chips.

221
00:15:33.858 --> 00:15:38.253
A curated type field (or a query classifier trained on clicks) fixed it:

222
00:15:38.533 --> 00:15:43.162
0.950, then 1.000 in L1.

223
00:15:44.512 --> 00:15:49.355
Spell correction from the query log (did-you-mean), rather than blind fuzzy matching.

224
00:15:49.635 --> 00:15:51.235
Guard rails on fuzziness:

225
00:15:51.515 --> 00:15:53.243
No fuzziness for short terms:

226
00:15:53.523 --> 00:15:59.773
Lucene's AUTO fuzziness allows 0 edits for 1 to 2 chars and 1 edit for 3 to 5.

227
00:16:00.053 --> 00:16:02.933
Penalise fuzzy matches below exact matches.

228
00:16:03.213 --> 00:16:05.389
Never fuzz brand names or numbers.

229
00:16:06.739 --> 00:16:15.891
L1's naive fuzzy expanded chai, then chaki (Chakki Atta) because chai isn't in the product vocabulary (only its synonym tea is).

230
00:16:17.241 --> 00:16:19.567
Business ranking, filters and facets.

231
00:16:19.847 --> 00:16:22.023
Text relevance gets you the candidates.

232
00:16:22.303 --> 00:16:34.783
The final order also uses business signals: in stock at the customer's dark store;. popularity and conversion;. margin and promotions (with care);. personalisation (past purchases).

233
00:16:36.133 --> 00:16:47.290
Function score: final = textscore times f(popularity) times stockfactor, or a learned model (learning to rank) re-ranking the top 100 to 1,000.

234
00:16:47.570 --> 00:17:05.874
L1: multiplying by 1 + 0.1, ln(1 pluspopularity) and demoting out-of-stock times 0.3 took out-of-stock in the top 3 from 11 of 60 to 0, with nDCG 1.000, then 0.992.

235
00:17:06.154 --> 00:17:07.733
Filters versus queries:

236
00:17:08.013 --> 00:17:15.863
Filters (category = Masala, instock = true, storeid =...) don't affect scores and can be cached as bitsets.

237
00:17:16.143 --> 00:17:17.189
Queries score.

238
00:17:17.469 --> 00:17:19.495
Put structured constraints in filters.

239
00:17:20.845 --> 00:17:30.147
Facets: counts per attribute value for the current result set (Masala 1,022, Snacks 240), computed from doc values.

240
00:17:30.427 --> 00:17:31.941
They're shown for refinement.

241
00:17:32.221 --> 00:17:34.824
L1, L2 and L3 all show them.

242
00:17:35.104 --> 00:17:43.744
In L3, Meilisearch's estimatedTotalHits capped at 1,000 while the facet counts summed to 1,262.

243
00:17:44.024 --> 00:17:45.965
Know your engine's approximations.

244
00:17:47.315 --> 00:17:49.513
Autocomplete and typo tolerance.

245
00:17:49.793 --> 00:17:51.137
Here is a quick table:

246
00:17:51.417 --> 00:17:53.273
Technique, and How, and Trade-off.

247
00:17:53.553 --> 00:17:55.622
Prefix query on the term dictionary.

248
00:17:55.902 --> 00:17:56.926
How: sham.

249
00:17:57.206 --> 00:17:58.102
Trade-off:

250
00:17:58.382 --> 00:18:01.049
Simple; can be slow for very short prefixes.

251
00:18:01.329 --> 00:18:03.121
Edge n-grams at index time.

252
00:18:03.401 --> 00:18:05.641
How: shampoo, then sh, sha, sham,...

253
00:18:05.921 --> 00:18:06.817
Trade-off:

254
00:18:07.097 --> 00:18:08.910
Fast queries; bigger index.

255
00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:11.387
Completion suggester (FST).

256
00:18:11.667 --> 00:18:12.329
How:

257
00:18:12.609 --> 00:18:15.126
A dedicated in-memory structure with weights.

258
00:18:15.406 --> 00:18:16.302
Trade-off:

259
00:18:16.582 --> 00:18:19.675
Fastest; separate field, no filters (or limited).

260
00:18:19.955 --> 00:18:22.473
Trigram similarity (pgtrgm).

261
00:18:22.753 --> 00:18:23.414
How:

262
00:18:23.694 --> 00:18:25.315
Similarity of trigram sets.

263
00:18:25.595 --> 00:18:26.491
Trade-off:

264
00:18:26.771 --> 00:18:29.907
Typo-tolerant; PostgreSQL-native (L2).

265
00:18:30.187 --> 00:18:32.107
Search-as-you-type from a query log.

266
00:18:32.387 --> 00:18:33.049
How:

267
00:18:33.329 --> 00:18:35.718
Suggest popular queries, not products.

268
00:18:35.998 --> 00:18:36.894
Trade-off:

269
00:18:37.174 --> 00:18:39.649
Best UX; needs logs and moderation.

270
00:18:40.999 --> 00:18:47.100
Latency budget: autocomplete fires on every keystroke, so target 50 ms end to end.

271
00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:54.996
Debounce on the client (100 to 150 ms), cache popular prefixes, and cancel superseded requests.

272
00:18:56.346 --> 00:18:58.287
Search inside PostgreSQL.

273
00:18:58.567 --> 00:19:08.935
PostgreSQL is a reasonable first search engine (L2, 200,000 rows): pgtrgm + GIN for substring and fuzzy match:

274
00:19:09.215 --> 00:19:27.135
ILIKE '%paneer%' 28, then 14 ms (11 MB index). wordsimilarity('shampo', name) finds Shampoo at 0.86. tsvector + GIN with websearchtotsquery, tsrank and weights (A for name, B for brand or category).

275
00:19:27.415 --> 00:19:31.575
The english config stems tomatoes and even tomatos to tomato.

276
00:19:31.855 --> 00:19:34.842
Facets with GROUP BY GROUPING SETS in one round trip.

277
00:19:35.122 --> 00:19:35.954
Limits:

278
00:19:37.304 --> 00:19:44.536
No transliteration or Hindi stemming out of the box: आटा gave an empty query and aata 0 rows.

279
00:19:44.816 --> 00:19:48.144
Relevance tuning is coarser than in a dedicated engine.

280
00:19:48.424 --> 00:19:51.389
Search load competes with OLTP (Ch 21).

281
00:19:51.669 --> 00:19:54.528
A good fit for admin tools and small catalogs.

282
00:19:54.808 --> 00:19:58.413
Customer-facing search at scale moves to a dedicated engine.

283
00:19:59.763 --> 00:20:01.235
Keeping the index in sync.

284
00:20:01.515 --> 00:20:05.611
The search index is a derived store, a read model (Ch 15).

285
00:20:05.891 --> 00:20:08.793
The source of truth stays in the service databases.

286
00:20:09.073 --> 00:20:10.054
The patterns:

287
00:20:11.404 --> 00:20:12.748
Here is a quick table:

288
00:20:13.028 --> 00:20:15.289
Pattern, and Consistency, and Notes.

289
00:20:15.569 --> 00:20:18.321
Dual write (service writes DB, then index).

290
00:20:18.601 --> 00:20:19.625
Consistency:

291
00:20:19.905 --> 00:20:22.529
Can diverge on any failure (Ch 13).

292
00:20:22.809 --> 00:20:23.620
Notes:

293
00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:24.561
Don't.

294
00:20:24.841 --> 00:20:27.892
Outbox or CDC, then Kafka, then indexer.

295
00:20:28.172 --> 00:20:29.196
Consistency:

296
00:20:29.476 --> 00:20:31.823
Eventually consistent, ordered per key.

297
00:20:32.103 --> 00:20:32.913
Notes:

298
00:20:33.193 --> 00:20:34.644
The standard (L3).

299
00:20:34.924 --> 00:20:36.481
Periodic full reindex.

300
00:20:36.761 --> 00:20:37.785
Consistency:

301
00:20:38.065 --> 00:20:39.345
Stale between runs.

302
00:20:39.625 --> 00:20:40.436
Notes:

303
00:20:40.716 --> 00:20:42.935
Also needed for rebuilds and backfills.

304
00:20:44.285 --> 00:20:46.205
The rules L3 demonstrates:

305
00:20:46.485 --> 00:20:47.701
Batch and pipeline.

306
00:20:47.981 --> 00:20:51.586
Engines have high per-request overhead (segment refresh, commit).

307
00:20:51.866 --> 00:20:59.333
An indexer that waited for each 20-document task lagged 22 s (p50) at 200 updates per second.

308
00:20:59.613 --> 00:21:03.901
Pipelined (enqueue and continue), lag was ~0.8 s.

309
00:21:04.181 --> 00:21:05.887
Preserve order per document.

310
00:21:06.167 --> 00:21:14.765
Two indexers taking alternating chunks of the outbox applied 26 older versions over newer ones and left 3 of 20 products stale.

311
00:21:15.045 --> 00:21:19.887
Partitioning by product id (Kafka key = product id) gave 0.

312
00:21:21.237 --> 00:21:28.235
Engines with external versioning (Elasticsearch versiontype=external) can also reject older writes.

313
00:21:28.515 --> 00:21:32.803
Deletes and unpublishes must flow through the same path (tombstone events).

314
00:21:33.083 --> 00:21:38.629
Joins: search documents are denormalised (product + brand + category + stock).

315
00:21:38.909 --> 00:21:45.437
A change to a brand name must fan out to all its products, which is a reindex job, not a per-event update.

316
00:21:46.787 --> 00:21:55.086
Fast-changing, per-store data (stock) is often kept out of the document and applied as a filter from a separate store at query time.

317
00:21:56.436 --> 00:21:58.164
Reindexing without downtime.

318
00:21:58.444 --> 00:22:02.028
Analyzer or mapping changes require a full reindex.

319
00:22:02.308 --> 00:22:03.332
The process:

320
00:22:03.612 --> 00:22:06.215
Build productsv2 alongside productsv1.

321
00:22:06.495 --> 00:22:14.068
Replay recent changes into both (dual indexing from the stream, or catch-up from the outbox position recorded at the start).

322
00:22:14.348 --> 00:22:19.596
Validate: doc counts, judged-query nDCG, a shadow query comparison.

323
00:22:19.876 --> 00:22:26.148
Atomically switch an alias (Elasticsearch or OpenSearch aliases, Meilisearch swap-indexes).

324
00:22:26.428 --> 00:22:28.860
Keep v1 for rollback, then delete it.

325
00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:41.239
L3: rebuild + swap in ~5 s while four clients searched continuously, with 6,604 successful queries, 0 empty, 0 errors.

326
00:22:42.589 --> 00:22:44.189
Vector search and ANN.

327
00:22:44.469 --> 00:22:50.357
Embedding models map text (and images) to vectors, so that semantically similar items are close.

328
00:22:50.637 --> 00:22:54.840
That catches healthy breakfast, then oats and poha, which share no words.

329
00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:58.896
Search becomes nearest neighbours by cosine or dot product.

330
00:22:59.176 --> 00:23:01.949
Exact k-NN is O(N, d) per query.

331
00:23:02.229 --> 00:23:03.104
L4:

332
00:23:03.384 --> 00:23:11.811
100,000 times 64 dims took ~4 ms or query, fine for small collections and too slow at 50M.

333
00:23:12.091 --> 00:23:14.032
Approximate NN (ANN):

334
00:23:14.312 --> 00:23:18.493
HNSW: a multi-layer proximity graph searched greedily.

335
00:23:18.773 --> 00:23:24.768
The default in Lucene or OpenSearch k-NN, pgvector, Qdrant, Weaviate and Milvus.

336
00:23:26.118 --> 00:23:28.422
IVF: cluster and search the nearest lists.

337
00:23:28.702 --> 00:23:31.155
Product quantisation compresses vectors.

338
00:23:31.435 --> 00:23:33.227
HNSW parameters:

339
00:23:33.507 --> 00:23:42.083
M (links per node): memory and recall. efConstruction: build time and graph quality. efSearch: query time versus recall.

340
00:23:42.363 --> 00:23:43.707
L4, from scratch:

341
00:23:45.057 --> 00:23:46.401
Here is a quick table:

342
00:23:46.681 --> 00:23:51.695
Neighbour selection, and efSearch, and Recall@10, and Speed versus brute force.

343
00:23:51.975 --> 00:23:54.279
Simple (closest M). efSearch:

344
00:23:54.559 --> 00:23:55.348
40.

345
00:23:55.628 --> 00:23:56.759
Recall@10:

346
00:23:57.039 --> 00:23:58.681
0.872.

347
00:23:58.961 --> 00:24:00.497
Speed versus brute force:

348
00:24:00.777 --> 00:24:01.951
33x.

349
00:24:02.231 --> 00:24:03.553
Simple. efSearch:

350
00:24:03.833 --> 00:24:05.092
160.

351
00:24:05.372 --> 00:24:06.503
Recall@10:

352
00:24:06.783 --> 00:24:08.489
0.945.

353
00:24:08.769 --> 00:24:10.305
Speed versus brute force:

354
00:24:10.585 --> 00:24:11.652
11x.

355
00:24:11.932 --> 00:24:14.940
Heuristic (paper's Algorithm 4). efSearch:

356
00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:16.009
40.

357
00:24:16.289 --> 00:24:17.420
Recall@10:

358
00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:19.300
0.991.

359
00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:21.116
Speed versus brute force:

360
00:24:21.396 --> 00:24:22.569
34x.

361
00:24:22.849 --> 00:24:24.364
Heuristic. efSearch:

362
00:24:24.644 --> 00:24:25.433
80.

363
00:24:25.713 --> 00:24:26.844
Recall@10:

364
00:24:27.124 --> 00:24:28.916
1.000.

365
00:24:29.196 --> 00:24:30.732
Speed versus brute force:

366
00:24:31.012 --> 00:24:31.951
20x.

367
00:24:33.301 --> 00:24:43.839
The neighbour-selection heuristic (prefer links in diverse directions) was the difference between a plateau at 0.945 and 1.000.

368
00:24:44.119 --> 00:24:48.898
Implementation details matter, which is one reason to use a mature library.

369
00:24:49.178 --> 00:24:57.797
Memory: vectors (100K times 64 times 4 B = 25 MB) plus graph links (~10 MB).

370
00:24:58.077 --> 00:25:04.178
At 768 dims and 50M items that's ~150 GB of floats.

371
00:25:04.458 --> 00:25:10.645
Quantisation (int8, binary) and disk-based indexes (DiskANN) exist for that reason.

372
00:25:10.925 --> 00:25:13.847
Filtering + ANN (in-stock, store) is hard.

373
00:25:14.127 --> 00:25:19.205
Pre-filtering breaks graph connectivity, and post-filtering may return too few results.

374
00:25:19.485 --> 00:25:23.538
Engines offer filtered HNSW or adaptive strategies.

375
00:25:23.818 --> 00:25:26.058
Test with realistic filter selectivity.

376
00:25:27.408 --> 00:25:28.517
Hybrid search.

377
00:25:28.797 --> 00:25:31.763
Lexical and semantic retrieval fail differently:

378
00:25:32.043 --> 00:25:40.363
BM25 is excellent at exact tokens (brands, sizes, SKU codes, atta 5kg), and blind to meaning.

379
00:25:40.643 --> 00:25:46.189
Vectors are good at intent (party snacks), and weak at exact attributes and rare terms.

380
00:25:46.469 --> 00:25:48.517
Hybrid runs both and fuses them.

381
00:25:48.797 --> 00:25:51.528
Reciprocal Rank Fusion is the common default:

382
00:25:52.878 --> 00:25:53.795
On screen:

383
00:25:54.075 --> 00:26:01.521
RRF(d) = Σ over retrievers r: wr / (k + rankr(d)) with k about 60.

384
00:26:04.371 --> 00:26:10.493
L4 (with toy hand-written concept embeddings, so the shape of the result is the point, not the numbers):

385
