00:26:55.617 --> 00:26:59.820
Plain RRF diluted the exact queries, and weighting restored them.

408
00:27:00.100 --> 00:27:01.871
Tune weights on held-out queries:

409
00:27:02.151 --> 00:27:06.417
L4 tuned on the same queries it reports, which overstates the gain.

410
00:27:06.697 --> 00:27:15.892
Beyond RRF: score normalisation plus a linear blend, or a cross-encoder or learning-to-rank model re-ranking the fused top 100.

411
00:27:17.242 --> 00:27:18.501
Measuring relevance.

412
00:27:18.781 --> 00:27:19.634
Offline:

413
00:27:19.914 --> 00:27:28.533
A judged query set: queries sampled from logs (head, torso, tail, zero-result), with graded judgments (0 or 1 or 2).

414
00:27:28.813 --> 00:27:34.893
Metrics: nDCG@k, precision@k, recall@k, MRR, zero-result rate.

415
00:27:35.173 --> 00:27:38.991
Run it in CI on every analyzer, synonym or boost change.

416
00:27:39.271 --> 00:27:41.511
That's what every table in this chapter does.

417
00:27:41.791 --> 00:27:42.645
Online:

418
00:27:43.995 --> 00:27:51.653
Click-through rate, add-to-cart rate from search, searches per session (reformulations), time to first add, revenue per search.

419
00:27:51.933 --> 00:27:58.888
A or B tests, or interleaving (mixing two rankers' results in one list, which is more sensitive with less traffic).

420
00:27:59.168 --> 00:27:59.915
Beware:

421
00:28:00.195 --> 00:28:03.160
Position bias in clicks (use it or correct it).

422
00:28:03.440 --> 00:28:05.019
Judged sets that go stale.

423
00:28:06.369 --> 00:28:08.097
Overfitting to your own judgments.

424
00:28:08.377 --> 00:28:13.219
L1 reached 1.000 on queries its author also wrote.

425
00:28:13.499 --> 00:28:18.129
Real sets need many more queries, fresh samples and independent judges.

