745
00:56:19.673 --> 00:56:20.868
ANN trade-off:

746
00:56:21.148 --> 00:56:24.945
With the simple keep the M closest neighbour lists, recall plateaus:

747
00:56:25.225 --> 00:56:31.433
0.945 at efSearch 160, where the graph clusters and search gets stuck.

748
00:56:31.713 --> 00:56:39.863
The paper's heuristic keeps a neighbour only if it's closer to the node than to already-kept neighbours, so links point in diverse directions.

749
00:56:40.143 --> 00:56:51.428
That reaches 0.991 at efSearch 40 (34x faster than brute force) and 1.000 at 80, for 50 % more build time.

750
00:56:52.778 --> 00:57:02.250
Use mature libraries (Lucene HNSW, FAISS, hnswlib, pgvector), and still measure recall on your data and filters.

751
00:57:02.530 --> 00:57:03.341
Hybrid:

752
00:57:03.621 --> 00:57:09.935
BM25 is perfect on the lexical set and weak on intents (0.542).

753
00:57:10.215 --> 00:57:15.762
Vectors are the reverse (0.289 versus 0.740).

754
00:57:16.042 --> 00:57:23.871
Plain RRF helps intents (0.769) but dilutes exact queries (0.903).

755
00:57:24.151 --> 00:57:34.327
Down-weighting the vector list keeps exact queries at 1.000 and lifts intents to 0.797, better than either retriever alone.

756
00:57:35.677 --> 00:57:38.493
The weight was tuned on the same queries it reports.

757
00:57:38.773 --> 00:57:43.232
In production, tune on a held-out set and confirm online with interleaving.

